I now have a comprehensive picture of the latest AI developments from multiple major tech news sources (TechCrunch, The Verge, VentureBeat). Here's my summary:

## Latest Developments in Artificial Intelligence (as of mid-August 2026)

### 🚀 Major Model & Product Launches
- **OpenAI "Ultrafast" mode**: OpenAI rolled out a new mode making its flagship model **GPT-5.6 Sol** run at **14x speed** (up to 750 tokens/second), powered by a partnership with chipmaker Cerebras. Targeted at incident response, customer service, financial analysis, and e-commerce.
- **Google Gemini 3.7 Flash**: Now available in AI Mode for Search, powering the Gemini Spark agent with "substantial" improvements in software engineering and web development. Google also added the ability to **remove visible watermarks** from AI generations.
- **xAI Grok 4.6**: Launched with frontier-level intelligence, major gains over the previous generation, and stronger long-running agent behavior. Grok Bot packages computer use, persistence, workflow learning, and multi-agent coordination into a "workforce interface."
- **Writer's Palmyra X6**: Reports 52% lower operating costs, 48% faster speed, and 10% quality improvement for its agent product.

### 💰 Corporate & Industry Moves
- **SpaceX closed its $60 billion acquisition of Cursor**, the AI coding tool, which will now work on Grok.
- **Stripe reportedly to acquire AI gateway startup OpenRouter for $7B+**.
- **OpenAI** reportedly disbanded its preparedness (safety) team, is losing executives (hiring a new CRO), and its **enterprise revenue has surpassed consumer revenue** as it heads toward an IPO.
- **Microsoft** is merging its Copilot apps into a "super app" and killing off unsuccessful AI features.
- **IBM partnered with OpenAI** to bolster its enterprise AI push.
- **Mistral AI** announced an ambitious plan to build 1 gigawatt of European AI compute by 2030 (~$38B in capital).
- **Nvidia** unveiled a new **$500B plan**, especially aimed at aging GPUs.
- Big funding rounds: **Cognition** reportedly raising at a $40B valuation, **Databricks** raising $5B at $190B, **Lovable** at $13.3B, and **Wispr** at $2B.

### 🤖 AI Agents & New Capabilities
- **Anthropic's "Claude Cowork"** (upgraded Chrome extension) saves chats to history and syncs across desktop, web, and mobile.
- **ChatGPT's Computer History** now tracks clicks and keystrokes.
- **Google Meet** added AI notetaking for in-person meetings (records audio, generates transcript and action items).
- **YouTube's "Ask YouTube"** conversational AI search rolled out to mobile.
- A notable experiment: Anthropic set AI agents loose on the same task — **they started a turf war**.

### ⚖️ Safety, Ethics & Controversies
- **Anthropic detailed its invisible text watermarks** for Claude (based on Google's open-source SynthID-Text), drawing backlash from users worried about being caught using it at work or school. Anthropic's CEO called the AI backlash "fundamentally a crisis of trust."
- **Amazon/Twitch** began training generative AI models on streamers' content **by default** (opt-out only), drawing criticism.
- Articles warn that **"rogue AI" concerns are no longer science fiction**, with renewed debate over AI safety and calls to stay open amid rising concerns.
- A legal case emerged where a woman claims Grok was used to transform a childhood photo into explicit imagery.

### 🏗️ Infrastructure & Compute
- A massive **AI data center buildout** continues across the US, facing growing local opposition (zoning bans, lawsuits, community pushback).
- **Z.ai** reported 2,436 vulnerability findings across 269 projects from security work in China.
- **Nvidia's $500B plan** and Mistral's European compute push highlight the global race for AI infrastructure.

**Bottom line:** The field is moving at breakneck speed — frontier labs are shipping faster, more capable models (GPT-5.6 Sol, Gemini 3.7 Flash, Grok 4.6); massive consolidation and funding ($60B Cursor deal, $7B OpenRouter, $40B Cognition); AI agents are becoming practical workplace tools; and safety, watermarking, and data-privacy controversies are intensifying alongside the infrastructure buildout.
